text:
Choose your install — What each install makes public
**Static.** Everything the browser needs to run a turn is downloadable by anyone who opens the site:

- the index in `/rag/*`: your page text, the vectors, the manifest;
- the instruction the model runs under, including `prompt.override` and `prompt.extend`;
- the gate thresholds and the retrieval levers;
- the routes `/ai/v1/embeddings` and `/ai/v1/chat/completions`. They are public on purpose; the proxy bounds what a call can cost. Your key is not in any of them. Security (/guide/security) lists the exact rules.

**Server.** The page receives a small part of that:

- the page list and the sections, for the scope picker;
- the text of the chunks an answer cites, so a source row can show its passage;
- the stream of the answer.

These stay on your server: the instruction, the thresholds and levers, every API key, the vectors and the corpus, and the turn log.

Two limits of the server install:

- **Answers quote your pages.** A reader can still read any passage that an answer cites. The server install keeps the index from being downloaded in bulk. It does not make the content secret.
- **The index must not be published.** A site built from `public/` copies `public/rag/` into its output. `serve --dir` refuses to serve a directory that holds an index manifest. Behind your own static server, exclude the index there. See The server runtime (/guide/server#setting-it-up).

Choose your install — Who each install is for
**Static fits** a documentation site, a landing page or a help centre whose pages are already public, where you want nothing extra to run and keep. The cost is that the prompt and the thresholds are visible to a reader who looks.

**Server fits** one or more of these:

- a corpus you do not want to ship as files;
- a prompt, a canary or an internal reference that must not reach the browser — the leak guard (/guide/server-operations#leak-guard) also stops it leaving in an answer;
- per-client limits, a daily cap and a deadline per turn without a proxy rule;
- metrics you can scrape, and a turn log that feeds `traffic` (/guide/improving#traffic) and `dataset` (/guide/fine-tuning#datasets).

The server install adds a process to run, monitor and restart. Limits and caches live in the memory of that process.

What changes for the reader
Nothing in how the chat works. Two differences, both on the server install:

- there are no follow-up chips;
- the first question waits for your server instead of the index download.

A pasted key and a greeting are still caught in the browser, before anything is sent.

React — Mounting
```jsx
import { DocPilotPanel } from '@cloflin/docpilot/react'
import '@cloflin/docpilot/web.css'

export function Layout({ children, pathname }) {
  return (
    <>
      {children}
      <DocPilotPanel config={window.__DOCPILOT__} route={pathname} />
    </>
  )
}
```

The component renders one empty `div`. Everything visible — the panel, the icon
sprite, the selection popover — teleports to ``, so where you place it in
the tree does not decide where it appears. Place it once.

Web — Colours
`docpilot.web.css` is the panel on real values, with its own
`prefers-color-scheme` dark branch and no host mapping. On a site with its own
tokens, override the `--dp-*` set after it:

```css
:root {
  --dp-surface: var(--my-bg);
  --dp-text: var(--my-fg);
  --dp-line: var(--my-border);
}
```

The full table is on Appearance (/guide/appearance).

**`prefers-color-scheme` is the only signal this file has**, and a page pinned
against its reader's OS is exactly the case it gets wrong: a site forced dark by
its own stylesheet gets a light panel on a light-OS machine. Say so instead:

```js
mountDocPilot({
  config: {
    ...window.__DOCPILOT__,
    ui: { theme: 'dark' },   // 'light' · 'auto' — the default, which reads the OS
  },
})
```

A pin puts a class on `` and the stylesheet declares its own dark set
there, so it outranks the media query without you overriding a token. See
`ui.theme` (/reference/config#ui-theme).

**This stylesheet is a cascade layer.** Its whole contents are declared inside
`@layer docpilot`, as every stylesheet in the package is since 2.0. An unlayered rule beats a layered one whatever its specificity, so any
CSS your site already has wins over the panel's without `!important` and without
counting selectors:

```css
/* no layer, no !important — this wins */
.docpilot__send { border-radius: 4px; }
```

If your own stylesheets are layered too, order decides it, and the order is the
one the layers are first named in. State it yourself rather than depend on which
`` the browser reached first:

```css
@layer docpilot, site;   /* before anything else — your layers now win */
```

Configuration — chat.models
- **Type:** `string[] | null`
- **Default:** `null`

An **ordered fallback pool**. The transport tries the first, and moves to the
next on a rate limit, a retired id, a moderation refusal, a 5xx, or a 200 that
carries no answer at all. The model that answers is remembered and tried first
next time; the ones that refused are set aside for a minute.

It exists for **shared free tiers**, where a `429` says how many other people are
asking rather than anything about the model — so pinning one free id buys a panel
that works until somebody else's traffic arrives. It is `null` everywhere else on
purpose: on a provider that bills per token, silently moving to another model
changes what a turn costs without being asked.

**A list is something you write down.** Silence resolves to one model — the
provider's own default — and the two ways to get a list are both in the config
file: this key, or `chat.model: 'free'` for the shipped one.

```js
// the shipped free pool, asked for by name
chat: { provider: 'openrouter', model: 'free' }

// a paid primary with free understudies
chat: { provider: 'openrouter', model: 'anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6', models: ['openrouter/free'] }

// your own order, nothing else
chat: { provider: 'openrouter', models: ['openai/gpt-oss-20b:free', 'google/gemma-4-31b-it:free'] }

// and one model, which is what an omitted key now means
chat: { provider: 'openrouter', model: 'openai/gpt-4o-mini' }
```
